WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.603
This is the launch video for Edge0-145B.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:07.206
Seventy-five tokens a second, on a laptop chip.

00:00:07.586 --> 00:00:08.671
Now read the header.

00:00:08.671 --> 00:00:10.298
The build is called l twelve-eighty.

00:00:10.298 --> 00:00:11.653
Hold on to that number.

00:00:13.998 --> 00:00:14.862
Hi, I'm Corwin!

00:00:14.862 --> 00:00:17.165
Edge0 says this model runs on a laptop.

00:00:17.165 --> 00:00:20.909
I read every file header in the release to see what it is.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:27.018
Start with a hundred and forty-five billion parameters, as the card counts them.

00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:28.533
Most are experts.

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:32.911
A third is one lookup table, keyed by the last few tokens.

00:00:32.911 --> 00:00:35.466
The rest is attention, routers and projections.

00:00:35.886 --> 00:00:37.908
Now sort them by where they live.

00:00:37.908 --> 00:00:39.641
The table never leaves the SSD.

00:00:39.641 --> 00:00:41.951
A token reads sixteen small rows of it.

00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:45.231
The base is Qwen's Flash-Next.

00:00:45.231 --> 00:00:49.823
The routers I compared are byte-identical in Edge0's files, and the lookup

00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:52.119
table matches NVIDIA's copy of Qwen's.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:54.550
What changed is the expert pool.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.566
Every expert is narrower, seventy percent of the size.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.023
In Qwen's layer, a token two thousand five hundred and sixty wide

00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:06.210
goes straight into ten of five hundred and twelve experts.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.834
Edge0 puts a shared projection in front, eight of them per layer.

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:13.286
The experts now work seventeen ninety-two wide.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:16.366
A second projection widens the result back.

00:01:16.366 --> 00:01:18.265
Attention never sees the difference.

00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:19.439
The price.

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.961
Those projections are seventy-three million weights a layer, read for every token.

00:01:23.961 --> 00:01:26.976
The ten experts that fire are thirty-four million.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.458
So how does it run?

00:01:30.458 --> 00:01:32.903
A small draft layer guesses up to four tokens.

00:01:33.303 --> 00:01:35.854
The full model checks them in one pass.

00:01:35.854 --> 00:01:38.087
Nine gigabytes of dense weights, read once.

00:01:38.487 --> 00:01:42.154
Plus two point four gigabytes of experts the four tokens route to.

00:01:42.554 --> 00:01:44.919
The GPU computes the experts it caches.

00:01:44.919 --> 00:01:48.298
The CPU computes the rest where they sit, in RAM.

00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:50.325
The SSD only holds the table.

00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:52.668
About three tokens kept per pass.

00:01:52.668 --> 00:01:56.555
On a twenty-four gigabyte card with sixty-four of RAM, that is around

00:01:56.555 --> 00:01:58.174
a hundred tokens a second.

00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:02.468
Now the speed claim, from Edge0's own setup video.

00:02:02.848 --> 00:02:04.206
Sixty-nine tokens of prompt.

00:02:04.206 --> 00:02:08.282
Over a thousand a second of prefill, with a first token after

00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:09.640
one point two seconds.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.017
Fixed costs dominate a prompt that short.

00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:19.178
On that machine, the README's best row for the 145B is forty-six.

00:02:19.178 --> 00:02:21.165
The 120B reaches sixty-nine.

00:02:21.165 --> 00:02:24.642
And twelve-eighty is the 120B's latent width.

00:02:26.033 --> 00:02:26.781
Then memory.

00:02:26.781 --> 00:02:29.397
Both models are plotted at twenty-nine gigabytes.

00:02:29.777 --> 00:02:31.871
Edge0 is measured internal peak.

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:37.580
The release's own reading, with every expert in VRAM or RAM, is fifty-four gigabytes.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.244
Twenty-nine leaves most of the experts uncounted.

00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:45.263
The benchmark chart says every score is Edge0's own evaluation.

00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:49.957
Yet on four of the six benchmarks, every baseline equals Qwen's published

00:02:49.957 --> 00:02:51.396
card, to the decimal.

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:52.474
Sixteen of sixteen.

00:02:53.742 --> 00:02:57.686
The demos are real agent sessions: Blender, a game world, a video

00:02:57.686 --> 00:02:59.330
pipeline, and a circuit board.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.235
None of them shows a token count, a machine, or which build ran it.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.992
Edge0-145B is Qwen's model with narrower experts, run by Strata's engine on

00:03:09.992 --> 00:03:12.688
fifty-four gigabytes of VRAM and RAM.

00:03:12.688 --> 00:03:14.036
A good result.

00:03:14.036 --> 00:03:14.935
Not twenty-nine.

00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:20.968
Narrower experts, Qwen's routers, and a speed measured on the smaller build.

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:22.017
Thanks for watching!

00:03:22.437 --> 00:03:24.539
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:24.539 --> 00:03:25.139
I'm Corwin.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:25.440
Bye!
